<!--Post: 12935--><!--Time: 1113793911-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12935"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 17, 2005 - 8:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->On April 19th 2005 the preliminary hearing in State v. Dennis Rader starts.  I thought a &#34;mini&#34; criminal law class on preliminary hearings might be helpful.  <BR> <BR>I have,in the past, taught Kansas Crim. law, evidence, and Kan. Crim. procedure at Kansas University, Wichita State University, Butler County Community College, the National College of District Attorneys, and the Wichita Police Academy. <BR> <BR>I will share my own insights, explanations and opinions in response to questions if there is enough intrest. <BR> <BR>&#40;Lost Dog is Prof.Emeritus over liquid refreshments and parties.&#41;  <BR> <BR>Phoenix.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12935-->
<!--Post: 12936--><!--Time: 1113795556-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12936"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 17, 2005 - 8:39 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Lesson 1. <BR> <BR>     a. The DA make written allegations against a defendant, accusing the defendant of a specific criminal act.  The document is called a &#34;Complaint&#34; <BR> <BR>     b. The pool of witnesses from which the State intends to draw it&#39;s witnesses must be &#34;endorsed&#34; &#40;written&#41; on the complaint.  The State is given great leeway if they request to add &#40;file a motion to endorse&#41;additional witnesses. <BR> <BR>     c.  At a preliminary hearing the state must prove 1&#41; there is probable cause to belive the crime was committed, and 2&#41; probable cause to belive Dennis Rader committed it. They must do this for each of the ten alleged murders. <BR> <BR>     d. In weighing the evidence, the judge ignores evidence favorable to the defense, does not pass on the credibility of witnesses, and draws all inferences from the evidence in favor of the state. <BR> <BR>     e.  The defense is not allowed to use the hearing for discovery of the state&#39;s case, but they always try to.  Look for a fight over the scope of defense cross-examination. <BR> <BR>     f.  Because favorable defense evidence is disregarded, the defendant never testifies &#40;it would be malpractice}and rarely presents evidence. <BR> <BR>     g.  Nola will present an opening statement, it will be aggressive and &#34;in-your-face&#34;. She takes control of the entire courtroom if the defense lawyer lets her.  The personal battle will be lively. <BR> <BR>     h.  Scientific evidence can be presented by lab reports w/o the live witness at a prelim. <BR> <BR>     i.   Bill Eckert&#40;deceased&#41;did either all or most of the autopsies. His death creates an enormous hearsay issue.  Look for big arguments and objections in this area. <BR> <BR>     IMO The DA gets a bind-over on all counts. <BR> <BR>Questions? <BR> <BR>Phoenix<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12936-->
<!--Post: 12938--><!--Time: 1113801381-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12938"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 17, 2005 - 10:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix, this was so good I&#39;ve linked it and posted it - with plentiful attribution, of course - at my <a href="http://www.planethuff.com/darkside">blog.</a>  <BR> <BR>I for one thank you - good food for thought.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12938-->
<!--Post: 12939--><!--Time: 1113803860-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12939"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 17, 2005 - 10:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->P’x, <BR> <BR>Before I start pumping up the kegger, is this hearing really going down on Tuesday? <BR> <BR>Will the defense request &#40; and receive&#41; a new hearing date?  <BR> <BR>I can’t imagine they are too well-prepared. Are they required to present anything other than a plea? <BR> <BR>Knowing what we know of the evidence – DNA match, floppy disc, Home Depot cam, voiceprint match from the Fox call, possible leftover trophies from Rader’s home – it seems foolhardy for the defense to subject their client to this forum.  <BR> <BR>My understanding is that the defense reserves the right to waive the hearing. After reading your summary I have to wonder, Is there any benefit to the defendant in this process? <BR> <BR>One more question. Do you grade on a curve? <BR> <BR>CanisDefluo <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12939-->
<!--Post: 12943--><!--Time: 1113809562-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12943"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 12:32 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Cool.  This will make up for all those horrible recurring dreams where it&#39;s finals day and I skipped every class and can&#39;t find the room. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m interested in more detail on the hearsay issue of a dead coroner/M.E./expert/whoever.  It seems unfair that Rader and his ilk get a pass for outliving their pursuers.  I&#39;m sure that the law attempts to strike some sort of balance - but how? <BR> <BR>PHOENIX =&#62; &#40;secret Zodiac decoder ring&#41; =&#62; IMGRETA <BR> <BR>You can&#39;t fool me.  I listen to public radio.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12943-->
<!--Post: 12946--><!--Time: 1113815264-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12946"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 2:07 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->LD <BR>1&#41; Maybe.  If it was going to be continued, there should have been advance warning so families of victims don&#39;t get let down.  The court file showed it still on for tuesday as of saturday. <BR> <BR>2&#41; No, I only grade on the back straight. <BR> <BR>Pax: <BR> <BR>This looms as a big issue.  This case was worked by the senior people and its 30 years later.  They have to be subject to cross examination to testify. <BR> <BR>Maybe the next subject should be &#34;chain of custody&#34; &#40;how to handle evidence between the crime and the court&#41; <BR> <BR>Why have the hearing if the evidence is that strong?  Sometimes the evidence is wrong.  Sometimes it&#39;s right but its inadmissable.  This is a REALLY good question that deserves a better answer than I can muster now.  There are lots of sound tactical reasons why a defense lawyer does that.  I&#39;ll try to summarize them for you tomorrow. <BR> <BR>Steve H.&#34; Thanks.  Since &#34;catch&#34; appears to have  turned to &#34;caught&#34;, I thought trial analysis might be helpful.  <BR> <BR>Phoenix<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12946-->
<!--Post: 12949--><!--Time: 1113833415-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12949"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sidewalker<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sidewalker<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 7:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thank you Phoenix, this is great! <BR>I also have questions about the hearsay part sure to come up regarding the autopsy findings. <BR>Since its fairly obvious most of the victims were strangled, there isnt much do say about that part &#40;giving that kansas wont seek the death penalty, regardless if Rader tortured his victims&#41; <BR>I can see how chain of custody of the evidence against rader WOULD be important &#40;more so than coroners/dead autopsy guy, not being available to be cross examined&#41; <BR> <BR>Also, why would evidence be inadmissable &#40;in your opinion&#41; in this case?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12949-->
<!--Post: 12951--><!--Time: 1113834150-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12951"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lorenap<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lorenap<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 7:22 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My big question is will there be cameras allowed in the courtroom? Thanx.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12951-->
<!--Post: 12954--><!--Time: 1113840957-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12954"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 9:15 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix - You stinker! I new you had a criminal defense background. Remember - and you dismissed it! Here is what I got from my Blacks Law Dictionary: <BR> <BR>The reason for the hearsay rule is that the creibility of the witness is the key ingredient in weighing the truth of the statement, so when the statement is made out court, without the benefit of cross-examination and without the witness&#39;s demeanor being subject to assessment by the trier of fact &#40;Judge or jury&#41; there is generally no adequate basis for determing whether the out-of-court statement is true. <BR> <BR>There are many exceptions to the heresay rule of exclusion based on a combination of trustworthiness and necessity. Thus, official written statements, such as police reports, coroner report etc. are based on firsthand knowledge and where the officer/coroner has an official duty to make the report, and has no motive to falsify are admissable.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12954-->
<!--Post: 12957--><!--Time: 1113871587-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12957"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Brown_tornado<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Brown_tornado<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 5:46 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, Phoenix. I was wondering when someone &#40;with a law background&#41; would step up to the plate. A large slice of raisin pie for you. <BR> <BR>Paul<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12957-->
<!--Post: 12959--><!--Time: 1113874684-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12959"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 6:38 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky: <BR>guilty as charged. <BR> <BR>KSA 60-460: &#34;Evidence of a statement which is made other than by a witness while testifying at the hearing, offered to prove the truth of the matter stated, is hearsay evidence and inadmissible except:&#34; and there are 31 listed exceptions where the hearsay is reliable enough to be admitted.   <BR> <BR>The exception you are referring to is: <BR>&#34;&#40;m&#41;   Business entries and the like. Writings offered as memoranda or records of acts, conditions or events to prove the facts stated therein, if the judge finds that &#40;1&#41; they were made in the regular course of a business at or about the time of the act, condition or event recorded and &#40;2&#41; the sources of information from which made and the method and circumstances of their preparation were such as to indicate their trustworthiness.&#34;   <BR> <BR>The Kansas Supreme Court has ruled that hearssay may be used at the preliminary hearing, the problem here is that the statute forbids it.  Kansas is more strict in the use of hearsay than Federal courts. <BR> <BR>Recently our legislature enacted two statutes on admitting forensic reports w/o the examiner testifying live.  The problem is that they are specific to KBI, FBI, Forensic Science Center, etc.  Eckert&#39;s Halpern Center is NOT included. <BR> <BR>Plus, expert opinions &#40;forensic pathologists included&#41; are not automatically admitted as a business record. <BR> <BR>&#40;no offense lostdog, but this one shapes up as a major dogfight&#41; <BR> <BR>Phoenix <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12959-->
<!--Post: 12960--><!--Time: 1113879654-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12960"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 8:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix - BTW I work for KState, Washburn and Notre Dame alumni. Wow, the hearsay rule in Kansas is going to be a serious issue. Are you saying that case law is not going to set a new precedent with regards to the hearsay rule? Do you mean that the pathology reports would be excluded under the hearsay rule and the DNA testing would be admitted?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12960-->
<!--Post: 12962--><!--Time: 1113881696-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12962"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 8:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky: <BR> <BR>I&#39;m KU but hey, <BR> <BR>State v. Rader probably reconciles diverging case lines.  I’ve given this lots of thought.  Remember unsettled law favors the defense.  They want an argument while the prosecution wants certainty. <BR> <BR>Your analysis may well be correct.  This one’s a mess. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12962-->
<!--Post: 12964--><!--Time: 1113882896-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12964"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 18, 2005 - 8:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Actually, I think the strongest of the two will be the DNA anyway! <BR> <BR>BTW - I&#39;m a rock chalk jayhawk too! You should see it around my office during the basketball and football season, you&#39;d think the meglamaniacs could get along cause all their teams suck anyway! <IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0>~ <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
