<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12995"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 8:49 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Else - Did you survive this last blizzard? The motion is probably the coroner&#39;s reports - if Phoenix is right about the heresay rule. I would argue to the ends of the earth over that one. The coroner&#39;s reports should be viewed as an official document and therefore should be admissable regardles of the fact the coroner is no longer with us. I would think that he had assistant&#39;s that viewed each body as well. I&#39;m also guessing that the KBI was involved in the process and that would add weight to the admissability of the coroner&#39;s reports. I hope the Kansas Supreme Court&#39;s ruling will set the precedent with the hearsay rule.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
