<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12998"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 9:19 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky - I surrived.  I had just hoped it would have been just a little more snow tho...it would have been fun to take the extra days off, ya know? But still...it was great.  I love it here...you never know what the skies will bring next as you well know.  Did YOU surrive? <BR>Hey, also, I think it might have been on another thread..the telephone company here prior to changing to Qwest... <BR>it was US West Communications...I am pretty sure?????<IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0> <BR>...ALso, seems to me that Denny Boy is still trying to be &#34;in Control&#34; read the article on Kansas.com <BR>The FREAK <BR>It&#39;s good for him they put a bullet proof vest on him.... <BR>I just wish he would have made some kinda of move...a little fit throwing or something so they could have &#34;buzzed&#34; the crap &#40;literally&#41; right out of him.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12998-->
<!--Post: 13018--><!--Time: 1113969704-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13018"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 9:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t know.  It was a surprise to me.  The decision to waive a PH requires the same complete grasp of the data that having a hearing requires.  All sources seem to be saying that discovery is not complete. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m having a hard time figuring this one out. <BR> <BR>Phoenix<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13018-->
<!--Post: 13019--><!--Time: 1113971519-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13019"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 9:31 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I know there are rules with regards to the scope of discovery and timeliness. I believe it was posted here somewhere that the Defense lawyers had only received a drop in the bucket of discoverable material from Nola. This was just a couple of weeks ago.  <BR> <BR>How much evidence is needed to establish probable cause, and how much satisfies the discovery rule?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13019-->
<!--Post: 13021--><!--Time: 1113972866-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13021"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 9:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Second question first.  Kansas is actually quite progressive on its discovery policies.  The Sedgwick County DA maintains an &#34;open file policy&#34; counsel of record can get a copy of the prosecution file just by asking.  Unless there is a discovery order in effect this satisfies the rule. <BR> <BR>A discovery order changes the landscape; it becomes reciprocal.  The defense has to provide expert reports and other material to the prosecution. <BR> <BR>How much evidence?  very little.  Less than a preponderance &#40;civil standard&#41; and more than a reasonable suspicion&#40;stop &amp; frisk&#41;. <BR> <BR>The deal about the waiver, it&#39;s a tactical move; risk/benefit analysis.  Gains in information thats not in the reports is enormous, the downside risk is minimal. &#40;imagine a plaintiff&#39;s attorney going to trial on a med. mal. case with no depositions.  What do they tell their experts?  This is the same kind of thing.&#41; I&#39;m having a hard time understanding this. <BR> <BR>Phoenix<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13021-->
<!--Post: 13024--><!--Time: 1113975689-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13024"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 10:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with the idea of the defense putting on a strategy with the waiver. Do you think they are trying to send a mixed message to the Judge? Like &#34;hey we may have a client that is incompetent&#34; or &#34;it was his idea, and we can&#39;t get him to understand this process&#34;. <BR> <BR>Yeah, as for the civil part with regards to experts, if they are not mentioned in our Rule 26 Disclosures, then at some point they have to be endorsed whether it be by the defendant or the plaintiff and always after they have been deposed. <BR> <BR>Hey Phoenix - after you get with your Court house buddies tomorrow, let us know what they think about the waiver.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13024-->
<!--Post: 13026--><!--Time: 1113975860-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13026"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 10:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I heard that Rader is no longer in isolation but is in with the rest of the jail population. Is this true?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13026-->
<!--Post: 13062--><!--Time: 1114047926-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13062"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 20, 2005 - 6:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Great question, JOHNO. And if Rader IS in with the regular &#34;crooks&#34;, you would think they couldn&#39;t wait to beat the holy crap out of him. Especially since he&#39;s a known child molester, torturer &amp; killer! Maybe we&#39;ll get some &#34;good&#34; news tomorrow! <BR> <BR>Also PHOENIX &#40;or anyone else&#41;: Wouldn&#39;t the dogcatchers&#39; team benefit instead from a strategy that tries to hurry this thing to trial A.S.A.P.? I believe if he requests a trial &#34;Now&#34;, the judge has to grant it, correct? <BR> <BR>I remember reading in &#34;HELTER SKELTER&#34; before the trial, prosecutor Bugliosi wasn&#39;t even close to having his case together, since it was so convoluted &amp; involved. He was deathly afraid that Manson would want to start immediately, which would quite possibly have lead to his being found &#34;innocent&#34;. <BR> <BR>So Bugliosi &#34;bluffed&#34; Manson &amp; told the judge he wanted to go to trial ASAP. Manson fell for it &amp; told the judge he needed more time, which he got. He also got the &#34;death sentence&#34;! <BR> <BR>But isn&#39;t there a good chance that the Wichita DA&#39;s case is also full of major gaps, since it&#39;s so old? And wouldn&#39;t Raders&#39; team want to exploit this by demanding a trial &#34;NOW&#34;??<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13062-->
<!--Post: 13063--><!--Time: 1114049976-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13063"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 20, 2005 - 7:19 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I went back to where I read that and it was in an online CNN story from yesterday that said &#34;Rader, who is being held on $10 million bond, is no longer secluded from other prisoners.&#34; <BR>All the other stories from yesterday on the other news sources said he was being held in a single person cell away from the other prisoners. Maybe he is celled alone but is allowed in the excercise yard when other inmates are there. <BR>As far as the what comes next on the May 3rd arraignment, I think his lawyers will go for an insanity defense and he&#39;ll plead not guilty.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13063-->
<!--Post: 13064--><!--Time: 1114051300-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13064"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 20, 2005 - 7:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Vsc - Helter Skelter I read that book. Bugliosi was the first criminal trial lawyer to get a conviction based only on circumstantial evidence. Right Phoenix?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13064-->
<!--Post: 13067--><!--Time: 1114056360-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13067"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 20, 2005 - 9:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno: If a defendant is in custody, he has to be brought to trial within 90 days of arraignment. <BR> <BR>Rocky: Nah. Happens all the time.   <BR> <BR>There&#39;s a whole funky deal about what circumstantial evidence is.  If the dairy is sued for watering the milk, and the evidence is there are trout in the milk bottle, it&#39;s only circumstantial evidence.  If you find a box turtle sitting on a five foot stump, it&#39;s only circumstantial evidence that someone put him there. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13067-->
<!--Post: 13081--><!--Time: 1114092429-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13081"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 21, 2005 - 7:07 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix - Maybe you misunderstood me. Bugliosi set the precedent for a murder conviction with Manson based on circumstantial evidence. Ok that maybe a lil broad, at least in California he did. <BR>&#34;My constitutional law professor is next door, and he said I&#39;m accurate.&#34; <BR> <BR>BTW - I think you may know him. He taught at KU as well.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13081-->
<!--Post: 13083--><!--Time: 1114103371-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13083"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 21, 2005 - 10:09 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky: <BR> <BR>I did some fast and dirty research on circumstantial evidence in homicide cases in Kansas.  I quit at 1927 because my electronic database stops there.   <BR> <BR>“Circumstantial evidence is testimony not based on actual personal knowledge or observations of the facts in the controversy, but of other facts from which deductions are drawn, showing indirectly the facts sought to be proved. Circumstantial evidence is used to show the existence of a principal fact. A conviction of even the gravest offense may be sustained by circumstantial evidence.”: State v. Penn, 271 Kan. 561, 564,23 P.3d 889 &#40;2001&#41;,  State v. Clemons, 251 Kan. 473, 488, 836 P.2d 1147 &#40;1992&#41;, State v. Hanks, 236 Kan. 524, Syl.  3, 694 P.2d 407 &#40;1985&#41;.  State v. Henderson, 226 Kan. 726, 731, 603 P.2d 613 &#40;1979&#41;; State v. Steward, 219 Kan. 256, Syl.  13, 547 P.2d 773 &#40;1976&#41;; State v. Sparks, 217 Kan. 204, 535 P.2d 901 &#40;1975&#41;; State v. Ritson, 215 Kan. 742, 529 P.2d 90 &#40;1974&#41;; State v. Wilkins, 215 Kan. 145, 523 P.2d 728; State v. Hale, 207 Kan. 446, 485 P.2d 1338; and State v. Kennedy, 124 Kan. 119, 257 P. 726 &#40;1927&#41; <BR> <BR>Phoenix<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13083-->
<!--Post: 13085--><!--Time: 1114115567-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13085"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 21, 2005 - 1:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Fast &amp; Dirty? <IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0> Your not referring to West Law are you? HaH! I was taught circumstantial is any evidence that isn&#39;t direct evidence.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13085-->
<!--Post: 13088--><!--Time: 1114134698-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13088"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 21, 2005 - 6:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->No, I use Loislaw <BR> <BR>And real property is anything that isn&#39;t personal property?  You&#39;re right, but then what is direct evidence? <BR> <BR>Phoenix <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13088-->
<!--Post: 13108--><!--Time: 1114185831-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13108"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 9:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey Rocky and Phoenix...I am wondering something and you two seem to have the &#34;legal&#34; side of this down. <BR>What are the legal aspects...what will happen if the &#34;new rumor&#34; is true that the demon a&#42;&#42; has colon cancer? <BR>Other, than if he does, now the citizens of Wichita will also have bear the burden of medical expenses along with the &#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#39;s legal expenses. <BR>But what will happen in regards to motions, or plea&#39;s -  <BR>Thanks!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13108-->
<!--Post: 13109--><!--Time: 1114191904-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13109"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 10:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoneix - A trailer home that doesn&#39;t set on a permanent foundation is taxed as personal property and one that does set on a permanent foundation is taxed as real property. <BR> <BR>Direct Evidence: means evidence which in the first instance applies directly to the factum probandum, &#40;Lat. A fact which is in issue and to which evidence is to be directed&#41; or which immediately points to a question at issue, or is evidence of the precise fact in issue and on trial by witnesses who can testify that they saw the acts done or heard the words spoken which constituted the precise fact to be proved. Garner v. New Jersey Fidelity &amp; Plate Glass Inc.  Mo.App., 200 S.W. 2d 448 &#40;Blacks Law Dictionary&#41; <BR> <BR>Circumstantial Evidence: means the existence of principal facts is only inferred from circumstances. Twin City Fire Insurance Co., v. Lonas, 255 Ky. 717, 75 S.W. 2d 348, 350 &#40;Blacks Law Dictionary&#41; <BR> <BR>Else - Physical Incapacity, Physical Necessity, Professor?? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13109-->
<!--Post: 13120--><!--Time: 1114208939-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13120"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 3:28 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Rocky: How about a trailer home with a flat tire?  What if there&#39;s no spare and it&#39;s propped up on cinder blocks? What if there&#39;s no sewage connection but the owner takes a piss out the door?  Real or personal property? <BR>&#40;60 min to answer, student id# on each bluebook.&#41;   <BR> <BR>Else:  The sheriff who has custody of the inmate is financially responsible for the cost of medical care.  If an inmate needs a heart transplant, the sheriff pays for it. <BR> <BR>If the sheriff refuses to pay for the treatment, the defendant files for a Writ of Habeas Corpus alleging an 8th amendment violation &#40;cruel/unusual punishment&#41; and forces him to provide the treatment. <BR> <BR>Phoenix<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13120-->
<!--Post: 13124--><!--Time: 1114219492-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13124"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 6:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix - I think Else is worried that demon @&#42;&#42; is going to die before he goes to trial or gets convicted. What happens if he does start to get treatment for colon cancer during the course of the trial and he starts turning green. &#40;Like a dog shi&#42;&#42;&#42; razorblades&#41;? <BR> <BR>A - Personal Property<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13124-->
